NAME
       du - show how much disk space files and directories use

SYNOPSIS
       du [-s] [-h] [-a] [-c] [-d N] [--apparent-size] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       du adds up the space used by each FILE or directory (the current
       directory when none is given), including everything inside it, and
       prints one line per directory, deepest first:

           4.0K    /root/work/a
           12K     /root/work

       Without -h the numbers are kibibytes (1024 bytes). du counts blocks
       actually used on disk, so a small file still takes a whole block, and a
       hard-linked file is counted once.

       Use df to see how full a whole file system is, and du to find out what
       is filling it.

OPTIONS
       -s, --summarize
              Only one total for each argument.

       -h, --human-readable
              Sizes such as 4.0K, 12M, 1.5G.

       -a, --all
              Show files too, not just directories.

       -c, --total
              Add a grand total at the end.

       -d N, --max-depth=N
              Show directories at most N levels below each argument.
              -d 1 lists each subdirectory once.

       --apparent-size
              Count the file sizes (bytes in the files) instead of the space
              used on disk.

       -b, --bytes
              The apparent size in bytes.

EXAMPLES
       How big is /var/log?

           du -sh /var/log

       Which directories in /var use the most space?

           du -h -d 1 /var | sort -h

       Save the size of /home in kilobytes to a file:

           du -s /home > /root/home-size.txt

SEE ALSO
       df(1), ls(1), find(1), sort(1)
